[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04adb896-af69-53f9-996f-8c7be69fb4c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288066} {:answer/id #uuid "3a968a72-2876-5175-83e3-a654fd44f16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288437} {:answer/id #uuid "636c3ca1-d99d-5757-a094-7af9bbcd44fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288727} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b58fac-4a93-5253-ac67-bbc75bccb331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289480} {:answer/id #uuid "f13f1b31-b868-5297-b55a-d2aac128a689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a968a72-2876-5175-83e3-a654fd44f16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288437} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f54739-84a3-5784-b147-34f4eb92278d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c687bf7b-334e-54f3-b20c-cd4bb1895323" :question/math-academy-id "q-22826" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & >0 \\\\ 2 & >\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that $x>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bba0ad98-6940-502a-873c-8a589a03a499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "686d4b22-ca2e-59f6-8e25-257d6ecd36f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288763} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba0df17-086d-58ed-9b7d-f24d67addc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288793} {:answer/id #uuid "8c35509a-2cbc-54fd-8c0c-9fea55af851d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288970} {:answer/id #uuid "a424a5d4-9cbf-5918-9c70-6a17e021eef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289127} {:answer/id #uuid "de93cdc8-1f7d-5919-9d5a-9e6075b24bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c35509a-2cbc-54fd-8c0c-9fea55af851d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288970} :answer-field/id #uuid "2db6b84c-1bcd-5e77-b158-172c24231e44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca248134-22cf-5031-924c-1fcc047804dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-22979" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-{95}^{∘}?$\n\n- ${625}^{∘}$\n- ${160}^{∘}$\n- $-{455}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}:$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{95}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={265}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={625}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than ${625}^{∘}.$ So we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{95}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{95}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{455}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only ${625}^{∘}$ and $-{455}^{∘}$ are coterminal with $-{95}^{∘}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066b3f2a-935a-58e3-b45b-5ef8105ae921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288083} {:answer/id #uuid "4bddd408-461f-59df-971c-0fbf3e710d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288556} {:answer/id #uuid "5fda4d33-676b-57f0-8b2a-aebb545ffce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288702} {:answer/id #uuid "638e801a-59a0-5f14-bea4-c312ba4e168a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288730} {:answer/id #uuid "71f6b8a1-afac-5574-9400-c0d6dd93e7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f6b8a1-afac-5574-9400-c0d6dd93e7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288826} :answer-field/id #uuid "d43b6123-f31f-5681-9bc2-f17e4c41ee8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abc739c6-0758-56d6-9f78-098fd3207a39" :question/math-academy-id "q-2455" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)>0$\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3(1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9ad609-8d7b-5e63-94bf-7cfba3aec5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288460} {:answer/id #uuid "abf31e5b-9e7a-5b32-8ddf-d15f59773d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289170} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aa96ca-5fb9-50d0-8d56-f3836db4b900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289207} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a024b9-8648-5ee6-8075-33884e637425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289213} {:answer/id #uuid "e6200ebf-b364-55da-8734-0d50031c5a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a024b9-8648-5ee6-8075-33884e637425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289213} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ce7f3d-ede3-5d56-84d2-9fd025fc8f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54523433-2b3d-5350-9453-344ec229fc6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2469" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2992c7-e972-5221-9225-486c82bb8e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288320} {:answer/id #uuid "960ec2d8-c0c3-5467-ab3a-5e4c2284c0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289036} {:answer/id #uuid "c45268a8-6ed8-5a58-8765-0d9ba9986146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289329} {:answer/id #uuid "d80cd24f-1447-5b7c-8949-769b33ee4c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289469} {:answer/id #uuid "f615375e-48a5-5056-9f13-2c61679f1457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45268a8-6ed8-5a58-8765-0d9ba9986146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289329} :answer-field/id #uuid "90484cdc-753b-56f4-ad23-95386946959f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19694b62-f531-5e43-9d81-ba1b9a788be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-2471" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0$\n- $x=-2$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{2}+30x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}+30x & =0 \\\\ 15x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,-2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=30x+30,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =30>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =-30<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I is true while statement II is false. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is true while statement VI is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-2,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ac181e-f3e3-57ed-a551-0fcd69b656a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290861} {:answer/id #uuid "7379f244-251b-516e-bb6c-6df3e9750189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291127} {:answer/id #uuid "85773a88-ab71-5f2f-8b24-e2364651ad65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291200} {:answer/id #uuid "943f4957-8672-539c-a148-c12d80f86f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291275} {:answer/id #uuid "db61e708-0dd2-55c4-8d75-a8f5d1daa241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7379f244-251b-516e-bb6c-6df3e9750189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291127} :answer-field/id #uuid "312e558c-5174-5996-94d0-b061d662915f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c70458e-a9c0-5f53-8bd6-90f09521b402" :question/math-academy-id "q-253144" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}+5{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}={b}_{n-1}+3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=4{c}_{n-1}+7n$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term $7n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbb7da2-ea66-5b52-9302-ca4c39e2dbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290695} {:answer/id #uuid "52bb7bbd-6c3d-523a-be74-00838fa298d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290985} {:answer/id #uuid "80c32bd0-4cf5-53f8-8450-1957372f36f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291177} {:answer/id #uuid "9f75d5fc-5716-5e77-bda7-413b14e2eeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291323} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc3b14b-039b-55a1-b69c-a57544be0b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dbb7da2-ea66-5b52-9302-ca4c39e2dbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290695} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f38d93c-f65b-5a2b-8351-7ceddf8321fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e47f465-4e25-5a70-adfe-d172f722b8f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-253467" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{2}+3$\n- ${b}_{n}=2{b}_{n-1}-{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}={c}_{n-1}+\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${c}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00559cb2-bd4f-513b-ab1f-903cc3d78a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290637} {:answer/id #uuid "0770898f-538c-58c2-a7c4-9285df1937de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290669} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a6a26b-c115-5b9f-9483-8468b6cc30ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291358} {:answer/id #uuid "af09b7b4-5e81-533b-bd4e-4f8a438df128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291392} {:answer/id #uuid "d66f9e03-8e50-579d-ba70-ec9750819aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0770898f-538c-58c2-a7c4-9285df1937de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290669} :answer-field/id #uuid "72961321-d712-5e5e-a0d9-8d90041f3b8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1f62b55-725b-50fe-8589-865ff5ea3249" :question/math-academy-id "q-253468" :question/problem "Which of the following recurrence relations are first-order?\n\n- ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=3{b}_{n-2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{c}_{n-1}}{3}-2{c}_{n-3}$" :question/worked-solution "The order of a recurrence relation is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $1.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${b}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${b}_{n-1}$ and ${b}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $2.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${c}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-3}$ in the relation is $3.$ So, this recurrence relation has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ade459-9aba-5520-95d3-69ef3fd62d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d9f27e-7800-56b9-9226-098496e041e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290643} {:answer/id #uuid "26d10e33-bc36-5085-b0ed-e645cd08d368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290792} {:answer/id #uuid "59244b09-f996-5219-a0a1-da045f107124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291010} {:answer/id #uuid "7c1b5d38-5442-5da3-a331-b00127071a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291158} {:answer/id #uuid "ded3bfdf-b944-54bb-8e12-915290f48b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c1b5d38-5442-5da3-a331-b00127071a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291158} :answer-field/id #uuid "72169963-fa38-5472-934c-e80e3348c250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6aaec8bd-79a0-54bb-bcfb-1f08b1113c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-253485" :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}$\n- ${b}_{n}=4{b}_{n-1}+{n}^{2}$\n- ${c}_{n}=5{c}_{n-1}+6{c}_{n-2}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n)=0$ for all $n,$ and ${s}_{i}$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- ${a}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- ${b}_{n}$ is not homogeneous. The term ${n}^{2}$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- ${c}_{n}$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, ${c}_{n-1}$ and ${c}_{n-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45594262-3efe-57c7-b5b9-bfdbc6564c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290917} {:answer/id #uuid "7620126f-9b05-57e1-83c0-abc96aed6552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291136} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb07632-b195-5870-b2a7-66c1d64cb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291169} {:answer/id #uuid "a844a83e-0aac-539f-89a3-bca1ef965168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291368} {:answer/id #uuid "fba97015-0947-55e9-a31d-e68b17721999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb07632-b195-5870-b2a7-66c1d64cb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291169} :answer-field/id #uuid "966e70db-9154-5f74-a132-3c4d2bdeac39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bea0a781-9898-5e25-a447-5b87446089a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-2690" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ on the closed interval $-4\\le x\\le -2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $3$\n- The global minimum value is $1$\n- The global minimum value is $-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+12x+9 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =3(x+1)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n3(x+1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,-1.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,-2]$, the critical points are $x=-3$ and the endpoints $x=-4,-2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+3=-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+6(-3)^{2}+9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+9(-2)+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-4)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/fa/fa85119166ca325f9925ddd03a578fb5daae463c59c3b7b9f7ac0f6464a30241.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8f7ef5-274c-5a18-bf40-dbd12cbd1f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290684} {:answer/id #uuid "4931603f-83bf-5071-a3bd-c0b45754444b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290935} {:answer/id #uuid "b24ab148-c47f-5ea1-b796-e3809a66f34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291410} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f9769d-7490-55c3-9765-366a8800bb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291463} {:answer/id #uuid "c6025f92-8fc3-59aa-969a-c9a5d636c25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4931603f-83bf-5071-a3bd-c0b45754444b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290935} :answer-field/id #uuid "e349e4b2-4399-55f4-ab33-3c8c124c76c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e48a4a75-01e8-5c53-b3a8-5931680804d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-2691" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $4$\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-6x \\\\  & =2x(2{x}^{2}-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2x(2{x}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$ do not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-3(-1)^{2}+4=2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{4}-3(0)^{2}+4=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=f(1)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/a7/a7141a342d971d5b520b2f7f07f8fdb1c01ddca18a983fb0c83102a5bd4aee8e.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} {:answer/id #uuid "931ab8d4-be2c-5e4d-92d6-fc6433eec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291265} {:answer/id #uuid "9767e111-d1e9-5924-9dc2-6f58d60a1d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291290} {:answer/id #uuid "bb01b60e-6bb3-5d81-89d4-9256edcd3ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291443} {:answer/id #uuid "d869766d-8d2f-5348-b322-4dd0c0dc459d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15b4588-e9b5-5b8e-af3b-02394e3239c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69137884-05b3-53be-9c7c-95b53b1ed329" :question/math-academy-id "q-27009" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ \n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n- The function has a minimum value at  $x=2\\pi .$\n- The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c77bec4ebb04668fbff5176418ed9c09143a8660879fdf523a158c5f109006c.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ the function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a maximum at $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e0a734-5caa-53e6-95cb-8ebb089583f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295980} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dbaf5a-ef2e-5a0c-af17-283db49b71b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296204} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f54506-f8f9-5e84-a336-30e0e1061dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296499} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf5e982-270e-5ac6-907b-4a07fbb8da8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296515} {:answer/id #uuid "ed52198b-4523-5c6a-85f8-e3abbb92970e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1dbaf5a-ef2e-5a0c-af17-283db49b71b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296204} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ae0b34-6270-5c87-9457-d86beb87e7e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4be11add-d875-5219-b83f-33d16bf37abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-28675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $\n- $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c8ed87-cb43-5db0-be5c-e831e6121bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295822} {:answer/id #uuid "70fd28f2-8ceb-5fae-b21c-0d8c37373df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "992814b5-63ad-57d1-9bda-8a6d0aea5b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296088} {:answer/id #uuid "efc85614-0678-5ede-96c5-eeebb1586b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296543} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc665d1-d05c-5d6a-bc20-c9ca8c023c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efc85614-0678-5ede-96c5-eeebb1586b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a661465-5e98-54ef-82ac-034599d77271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b892079-10a1-5d68-a34f-01695fac566e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288066" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-3u+4=-3u-1$\n- $2u+5=2u+8$\n- $6u-2=3u+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+4 & =-3u-1 \\\\ -3u+4+3u & =-3u-1+3u \\\\ 4 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $-3u+4=-3u-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+5 & =2u+8 \\\\ 2u+5-2u & =2u+8-2u \\\\ 5 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $2u+5=2u+8$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6u-2 & =3u+1 \\\\ 6u-2-3u+2 & =3u+1-3u+2 \\\\ 3u & =3 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6u-2=3u+1$ has the unique solution $u=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e935809-7eff-5524-8f2e-8f4f21f5d685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295440} {:answer/id #uuid "276266b0-8b3d-5094-8f78-2c2d0df618f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295484} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc10199-d63c-5736-b2b0-1c0ef77650f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295796} {:answer/id #uuid "e00d1820-af62-5599-b6e8-6dccf62f726a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296455} {:answer/id #uuid "ff69cb28-f522-54a1-8193-c2a2130eae30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276266b0-8b3d-5094-8f78-2c2d0df618f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295484} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3788ac-8209-560a-917f-1cc30dcfba9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e1fdbd8-4927-548c-aafb-62f0ed007aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-288070" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-4y+2=-4y-5$\n- $6y+3=6y-1$\n- $5y-7=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2 & =-4y-5 \\\\ -4y+2+4y & =-4y-5+4y \\\\ 2 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $-4y+2=-4y-5$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+3 & =6y-1 \\\\ 6y+3-6y & =6y-1-6y \\\\ 3 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $6y+3=6y-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-7 & =2y+1 \\\\ 5y-7-2y+7 & =2y+1-2y+7 \\\\ 3y & =8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5y-7=2y+1$ has the unique solution $y=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f682ce-814d-576c-9d68-46eaffd55e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295506} {:answer/id #uuid "34d91129-90ea-5ce1-ba6a-b94c177abdbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295562} {:answer/id #uuid "5d99b17d-1345-5edc-934a-f0e572fd837c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b35403-5d7a-5721-b22e-eb61699d25cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296474} {:answer/id #uuid "f194dee2-3414-5e50-8807-b8317095ddfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d99b17d-1345-5edc-934a-f0e572fd837c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c9470b-a03d-5e8c-a6cc-e7fb12154e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22403a37-d9b4-5866-bee2-a28d9c27c3b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-288072" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $2z-4=2z+1$\n- $5z+6=5z+6$\n- $3z+2=z+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-4 & =2z+1 \\\\ 2z-4-2z & =2z+1-2z \\\\ -4 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $2z-4=2z+1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+6 & =5z+6 \\\\ 5z+6-5z & =5z+6-5z \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+2 & =z+4 \\\\ 3z+2-z-2 & =z+4-z-2 \\\\ 2z & =2 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3z+2=z+4$ has the unique solution $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ec15b0-9006-57c3-a6ed-6a7ffae1f559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295450} {:answer/id #uuid "483443d7-d949-50ec-a7b8-e99c24e9079f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295680} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0712b9-1553-53a1-83a6-c73d8f9df552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296012} {:answer/id #uuid "8f108ee3-0807-5637-839d-91c354641014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} {:answer/id #uuid "9d46e37c-b28f-5c45-9927-cdb6e5e1fe53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f108ee3-0807-5637-839d-91c354641014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8696e8-0827-512c-9081-1eb13fb3de19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae9bca83-e68f-5756-9519-1447b4343c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-288073" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $3w+2=2w+2$\n- $4w-7=4w-7$\n- $6w+5=6w+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w+2 & =2w+2 \\\\ 3w+2-2w-2 & =2w+2-2w-2 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3w+2=2w+2$ has the unique solution $w=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w-7 & =4w-7 \\\\ 4w-7-4w & =4w-7-4w \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $w.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w+5 & =6w+9 \\\\ 6w+5-6w & =6w+9-6w \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $w.$ Therefore, the equation $6w+5=6w+9$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d604b35-b182-51ef-8ad8-153a353f0418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2e8c0b-8871-52a8-8f7e-27c536e84a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295618} {:answer/id #uuid "560da999-55c4-5b56-a1d6-481d32f3a37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295765} {:answer/id #uuid "a943da63-fb2b-5fdc-9bc3-6074acd881a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296157} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c83eab-72fe-5dac-9261-1a12e8b33cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d604b35-b182-51ef-8ad8-153a353f0418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a845396-80e0-59b7-bded-5ac38a7616cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6480ee2c-c900-5448-85b3-32f94538971e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288074" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $9v-5=4v+2$\n- $2v+3=2v+7$\n- $5v-1=5v-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9v-5 & =4v+2 \\\\ 9v-5-4v+5 & =4v+2-4v+5 \\\\ 5v & =7 \\\\ v & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $9v-5=4v+2$ has the unique solution $v=\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+3 & =2v+7 \\\\ 2v+3-2v & =2v+7-2v \\\\ 3 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $v.$ Therefore, the equation $2v+3=2v+7$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-1 & =5v-1 \\\\ 5v-1-5v & =5v-1-5v \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $v.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b3b264-c3bf-56ca-98eb-2d6cd21c3128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee88acd-7941-541d-92ae-327eb954fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295723} {:answer/id #uuid "920b99e8-cc75-5101-9bba-6a0d521d38ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296052} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6ea962-b6ce-5bb8-b5bf-25d51a764721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296184} {:answer/id #uuid "f2861c84-edf3-5ebd-851c-60ce99e270e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b3b264-c3bf-56ca-98eb-2d6cd21c3128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c2d36a-6f01-5415-a69e-1a9749544603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5426119-4ea0-5c84-bb6c-cf460c622db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-288425" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- $6x+1=6x+5$\n- $7x-4=6x-6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2cb23e-a320-550f-80fb-5d57177ced2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295443} {:answer/id #uuid "5a31613b-8925-5a47-a2df-6b3f46bb6e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785} {:answer/id #uuid "7935fd04-86fe-5c9d-b74f-227c3df9fdc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295929} {:answer/id #uuid "e40fb2df-e9bd-55aa-a47c-3fc4ba486d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296484} {:answer/id #uuid "ef187862-df62-575f-8ffd-89e5afb16587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a31613b-8925-5a47-a2df-6b3f46bb6e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df2639b-eb03-5fd2-8163-d1dd5acd6070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31ef6d07-7ebc-5067-b0a5-3f361bec8d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $2x-5=x-2$\n- $6x+1=6x+9$\n- $-4x+3=-4x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5987c83e-257d-55b4-a17e-3965dafe501e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295783} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd73961-4fa5-55a6-b016-4ed50daa78c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295946} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5b127e-bdcf-5063-975e-7d2cfc4e8774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9ae661-533a-5359-845d-1a9edbbbdc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296526} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6316b3-6aa1-5dae-ba84-4457b158e8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6316b3-6aa1-5dae-ba84-4457b158e8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3dc02a4-0b73-558c-b7e7-05f609d48e61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43eccfac-bd80-5f7b-abff-3250eee5dc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $3x+2=4x+1$\n- $2x-5=3x-3$\n- $5x-4=5x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} {:answer/id #uuid "57f33323-a574-5d3a-9709-4eb09849a56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295774} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d54f7c-6d02-5810-afd3-16ba3cfaf37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296154} {:answer/id #uuid "bba10e7d-2cc2-5341-a2f3-b9fb123d6f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296250} {:answer/id #uuid "e870b774-8e7e-5746-ab82-10ba9e3d3195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d7bb9f1-b157-599d-93ed-6607f6670caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295620} :answer-field/id #uuid "543e005e-a445-5e44-847a-061f1c3bcb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $y>0$\n\nII. It is an increasing function\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064a647e-d06a-50bb-9cbc-0f17389eca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295298} {:answer/id #uuid "1066d92d-62c9-5dad-8b4c-dbbe71386d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3a0291-6ad9-5566-9fd5-0de08933b4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295630} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2ff4e9-ee34-51e6-b267-06c09d19013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1618392-44be-533c-81a8-efd84eeee3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n- $tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bef0bb8-217e-5b8a-a9cc-536ee2789431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297611} {:answer/id #uuid "0de244d0-0abc-5db8-b1f6-33c12e7e4d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297619} {:answer/id #uuid "4610e6ed-8488-5ffc-ac46-2ae33e6cf7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297840} {:answer/id #uuid "96337b72-0ea9-503f-87e2-d8f2d39190e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298201} {:answer/id #uuid "a14f5f06-92b3-58c2-b787-460bec0bf2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bef0bb8-217e-5b8a-a9cc-536ee2789431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297611} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6bb327e-5b1c-548b-8cdb-ae1ff55bd5a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70dbdf12-3bc6-5349-9efa-62768bd9b3d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318629" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}+y,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+2) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is not exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3xy,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy) \\\\  & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-4x)=4x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=6y-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21782e87-59b6-5912-b8b4-9fa6cf1b6342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297696} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc718c6-f925-5144-a9e5-17a853705577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297745} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0d2ffe-15cb-550f-9751-5852c9fb4b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298172} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9ca69b-44e0-51bf-82e4-b60846979e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a11aa3-7602-58a6-84d9-d87aaf9f82e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf9ca69b-44e0-51bf-82e4-b60846979e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298456} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbcc345d-f8ed-56e0-8640-dbcb0a23f4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "861223b8-6640-50cd-ba1a-46920dc4bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-318630" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$\n- $(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy+5,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+5) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+7) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{2}+x,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-2x)=2x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y-3x) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation III is not exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a25b1a2-e20d-5c53-8a13-6c9232a5dc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298008} {:answer/id #uuid "752fe78a-fefa-52a6-a225-a83083b62369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298058} {:answer/id #uuid "757d1ce4-f1bf-5fd0-804f-f3630bd25740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298061} {:answer/id #uuid "f385759e-cbba-56ac-bed7-1e81bce59c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298583} {:answer/id #uuid "f81ece97-48d2-5de9-a082-e3eacc213705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757d1ce4-f1bf-5fd0-804f-f3630bd25740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298061} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f65d6e-c5b7-516f-bba7-11721b3aeb79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a35d343-f492-5478-a8b0-3c966904f7aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31907" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bab4d3-6b3c-53df-9fc7-544789b896ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Y$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297570} {:answer/id #uuid "489f69c0-1a1b-59e7-8b51-291265d6c731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297849} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a73b98-3971-595d-8705-c31d2d15129f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298265} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b24876-2947-57fe-8ccd-af0c39bfc822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298343} {:answer/id #uuid "be22a610-f1a2-5929-9a8b-4e3c27a93183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b24876-2947-57fe-8ccd-af0c39bfc822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ and $Z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298343} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b416bba-d53a-5be8-8e38-203a53a4ab36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbe50951-f70c-5a66-becb-e9afd71bebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31968" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117151e4-84f1-51a0-ad37-2790a67d8c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300033} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef8e7de-cda4-5bc2-9a75-09cd3fe231de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300106} {:answer/id #uuid "559acb8c-35c5-53e0-8999-681587b19763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300407} {:answer/id #uuid "bb256cbb-a3a8-5890-9ba0-be61ca97e9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300900} {:answer/id #uuid "c17033bf-5f8f-5dc5-91b3-24397815b685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ef8e7de-cda4-5bc2-9a75-09cd3fe231de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300106} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a072265-5db1-5ee1-9458-23dd71204ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "775e3d7f-2959-5adc-b3eb-ee2a98bbde96" :question/math-academy-id "q-321646" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335a12ad-0dac-5924-8ffe-a33133e004e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300218} {:answer/id #uuid "43d9f0e8-cdc2-56a1-8bab-c00724f75170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300296} {:answer/id #uuid "4f638168-55fc-55a0-b8d7-14935a1f7909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300376} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaf1a59-f06c-5167-8a94-ef48da242119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300589} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7f8157-ed75-5b01-8f83-5eb9006ffeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eaf1a59-f06c-5167-8a94-ef48da242119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300589} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8e0352e-73bc-52cb-8b75-a00696bec104" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587dcbe8-780f-5ae0-a952-bc8f4458a90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32176" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30eb8020-4362-5dca-9aef-7187ca844777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300198} {:answer/id #uuid "53cd87ad-3a34-59b5-a960-6f170b23d525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300394} {:answer/id #uuid "704302a3-7420-5ad6-8aa0-cb9bb2d442dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300526} {:answer/id #uuid "725d1bc1-bca2-529a-9f96-435247a5b785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300531} {:answer/id #uuid "7c66242d-7e64-59c7-9be0-c1c3b331ab58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c66242d-7e64-59c7-9be0-c1c3b331ab58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300582} :answer-field/id #uuid "8960c85e-f54e-543c-bb0f-5279313d6604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1208e79a-a661-5205-bd4d-ce2da940348a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32177" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block lower triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6c2612-857d-542a-9a6b-ad1f47af45f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300172} {:answer/id #uuid "93d80384-ebe5-581f-ba4a-d8a5e20516f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300704} {:answer/id #uuid "9571f723-2f95-54ed-bbee-880c66f493ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300712} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c57575-0e5f-5557-ba3b-da777fe1bb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300776} {:answer/id #uuid "e2673918-0fa6-5a74-8010-7dbdc705928b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9571f723-2f95-54ed-bbee-880c66f493ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300712} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaed8cd6-2dad-51ca-9f3f-aa8b6a8316c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c780dd-c7ca-58af-8e2e-0a4e9d41e44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327582" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{8})$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(0,1).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d411a23-cef5-5417-ac73-cff86304f884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302644} {:answer/id #uuid "3640132a-8f13-5c3e-904b-39778c6e3de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302792} {:answer/id #uuid "a49d14e5-b780-5b98-be31-7d115025fd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303468} {:answer/id #uuid "a514caf8-f8b6-5221-937c-adcab645e60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303472} {:answer/id #uuid "e382c4b4-e686-51d2-beb7-5cafb3e78d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a514caf8-f8b6-5221-937c-adcab645e60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303472} :answer-field/id #uuid "100c3e25-c5ac-57fe-8297-0c550fe9a4ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f297359-1573-550c-acfb-39ada6af0bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-330297" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ffec46-af25-548c-b530-f028c09d9793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302789} {:answer/id #uuid "815db271-adae-55b8-bace-6da5e3ef1cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303263} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc0aecd-50d8-55d9-b9a3-af9953232c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303844} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f93721-52c4-554c-a99d-200f6b43b1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303863} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b33593-404e-56a3-a687-32d764b0ffe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b33593-404e-56a3-a687-32d764b0ffe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304020} :answer-field/id #uuid "c30f7306-2335-5f7a-8979-654d70343cd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78db71f4-3fc9-53f6-9a4c-5f66663f6749" :question/math-academy-id "q-330298" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $4$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106675f3-db71-5027-b3b7-f8833f64f184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302563} {:answer/id #uuid "229b952a-fc8e-5586-bfda-1680d8d306aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302675} {:answer/id #uuid "5f46ff7b-dc2c-5706-b853-9ed0f8d1eaba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303037} {:answer/id #uuid "badcbf32-c7d2-53de-a4f3-ed8437e856f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303610} {:answer/id #uuid "fd41e97c-ed05-5e99-9314-1d4e73072845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd41e97c-ed05-5e99-9314-1d4e73072845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304046} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8cb72c0-abe7-58fb-8b53-26c8e68b1182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50adfd6-221a-580f-9d34-b5717546927f" :question/math-academy-id "q-330300" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $5$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fce60c-20f6-5b64-96ff-f3f273ae9463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302769} {:answer/id #uuid "4987ef9f-dd17-58f4-8313-a94b824ccb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302919} {:answer/id #uuid "59618d1c-67e5-5572-aba8-d8372571bf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303001} {:answer/id #uuid "8535ddb6-6773-5f99-9bec-2b7887f28ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303285} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ba1f6f-70ad-5a51-8747-82113bdb911e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59618d1c-67e5-5572-aba8-d8372571bf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303001} :answer-field/id #uuid "541d9549-9edc-56d2-946a-2fb52062a7cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6d8d0be-fe4e-5e1a-948d-b8eb21d680a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330302" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce0f124-614f-5018-bf24-274425b0384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302939} {:answer/id #uuid "7760ac55-6d9d-5f25-a75a-d8143b980502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303193} {:answer/id #uuid "92bc0931-0ed9-5514-8f29-5214a0b79efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303368} {:answer/id #uuid "99d5125d-679c-51df-b3e6-3667846f3cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303410} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc7ca9-c17f-59de-a296-35d2471cf4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ce0f124-614f-5018-bf24-274425b0384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302939} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfc37eb7-c9b5-5399-8d2e-3637e84ad0f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63b3f264-c6f0-527c-811d-3ed89e69129d" :question/math-academy-id "q-330305" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e27b405-c7c3-5f62-a8f6-6a64cf338f01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302742} {:answer/id #uuid "63f3178a-b9c6-5d7e-b736-cc9281b3ce05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303061} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3afe43-bab1-5b9d-9b78-d4bcade00070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303336} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcca6c4-65bc-5bc2-8b5d-99e7b4b6dea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303825} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ca7eaa-ce56-541a-ab75-04508ebe8595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f3178a-b9c6-5d7e-b736-cc9281b3ce05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303061} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d2ea82-7e8d-541f-94a3-049caf6d6179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed465271-2eba-5278-8938-505251fa9eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-330308" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nI. $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n\nIII. $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff09b51-a998-59f2-b80f-ec8dd5327c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302560} {:answer/id #uuid "3f887f82-672d-59da-ad03-484f8f660797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302856} {:answer/id #uuid "7c36c13b-c34e-5b12-b9a9-ec4a81dec8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303226} {:answer/id #uuid "b0816ecf-db08-5968-b24e-af05713a4856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303544} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e636f4-c99d-5f7d-be29-44c525a2ab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f887f82-672d-59da-ad03-484f8f660797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302856} :answer-field/id #uuid "0225f4e6-2a7d-585e-9f5c-7e7304a3d60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7119481-1ac5-5669-b8e8-7afd953dbdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-330309" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option III is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536b00ce-ce2e-5ea4-a4fb-c820ac14e96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302974} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1732fe-1723-5267-989d-f733369e76d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303214} {:answer/id #uuid "e94231f7-23b2-525d-8155-f6679b777102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303909} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2f0c7e-3d6a-54da-94f6-7b7dd1e20601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303943} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1fb0dc-be08-5240-8989-5dc6857c1f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1732fe-1723-5267-989d-f733369e76d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303214} :answer-field/id #uuid "df82608b-0ae1-5687-8ecd-46a02fadb56d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891dbe2f-f88c-5e79-ae9d-64f7413a702a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330312" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={t}^{2}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e31168-b925-55a0-918c-6a38af46bc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302613} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5c738e-7eec-521a-97f4-0924a180ef65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302947} {:answer/id #uuid "52fb4f41-baa4-56a4-878f-9f64b18ce17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302970} {:answer/id #uuid "8390a730-eb52-524a-8a4d-b3c8c27dae1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303277} {:answer/id #uuid "c334134a-7cb5-54bf-b6ad-e3150bba6e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52fb4f41-baa4-56a4-878f-9f64b18ce17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302970} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d90b965-cdf8-5201-a449-7a708227a8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac4a66c-708b-59cc-844a-c54884651cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-330332" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$\n- $x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3ed5c2-1c9e-5734-aad6-8c9c21beef7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302656} {:answer/id #uuid "367e29de-e862-5fd8-9bd3-61eaa8ee74a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302793} {:answer/id #uuid "84f575b3-b359-5d38-86a0-403e93a5b541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303284} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7df275-3573-595f-b0e9-fa90bbdcf224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303526} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8f2e4e-26b6-5ea9-80c3-3285fc30d58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f575b3-b359-5d38-86a0-403e93a5b541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303284} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e5c2929-a9b4-560f-a877-a5e2f588c6b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89894e08-2da2-5128-a91b-b1054d28b3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330353" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8ac2a0-fa87-5056-972b-131bc1967d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303125} {:answer/id #uuid "b20ccc0e-1380-50a3-a249-a2c7d937e704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303558} {:answer/id #uuid "d98f18ae-3b52-5e3d-ba39-5b298d5c454f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303809} {:answer/id #uuid "f4eaa315-3d04-541e-9b40-dbca306a0d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303995} {:answer/id #uuid "ff282c72-ce1b-548f-aa07-a8228769755c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff282c72-ce1b-548f-aa07-a8228769755c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304065} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f5b2b5-c0fa-51a4-9270-eb12dfa9c461" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b7930e-1624-5550-b77a-3086492ab273" :question/math-academy-id "q-331060" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\n- $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$\n- $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$\n- $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,2+0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfc4cf2-a8e8-59f5-a6f8-ccb2a8fca6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302739} {:answer/id #uuid "41c868f4-3633-5cd6-abce-f793c2d3089c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302870} {:answer/id #uuid "9568a1c8-b5cc-544c-8e61-bd7731544f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303384} {:answer/id #uuid "cb37e7ac-a885-5c55-b66d-8c173d1e8048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303704} {:answer/id #uuid "fa36311d-8e86-5e73-8d31-3fce04a49a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb37e7ac-a885-5c55-b66d-8c173d1e8048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303704} :answer-field/id #uuid "155ccb92-44b7-540b-afa7-3a3cb56fda2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "363ad515-bb3d-517b-aff5-449014ea0698" :question/math-academy-id "q-331114" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} {:answer/id #uuid "a4be644b-3c50-5bb5-b637-a1dc0590c6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303470} {:answer/id #uuid "be5348b7-6521-531c-9590-b40f2257782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303623} {:answer/id #uuid "bface693-37fe-568c-a4fa-08c4b3ba5757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303630} {:answer/id #uuid "e424eab9-c2e2-5989-8b94-f4410f99f5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3bff16-f60d-5153-a071-915cdea9c3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcddfdb-c38a-5f92-b718-28036cb5e0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302637} {:answer/id #uuid "24531df4-5849-5e8d-b717-57d07a8550b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302686} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15be87-c9e8-5225-a673-4b26b316be25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303004} {:answer/id #uuid "66137ed2-2b14-58d0-bd8a-ff3d608e1ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303074} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d947d0-6f8d-54b2-932e-06f1d5e06c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7603a78a-aca0-51e5-9c68-1571861f143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303180} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9253d0-497c-5ed8-9904-752fb5b447a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303413} {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} {:answer/id #uuid "b8164d95-12bd-5b59-aa2d-33ca3cd95e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303598} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b02410-a499-5764-8c6c-bc4fea9102cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f51f02b-20a7-5356-a7bf-6883df31265e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7684d3-767a-52e5-bcc5-d780b7faa6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302753} {:answer/id #uuid "51c3b1e8-d753-5298-9a6f-e67e6cb118db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303225} {:answer/id #uuid "da2cbaaf-6e7b-5962-ac89-10a2621a85d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303814} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fad90-31aa-5644-9fc2-5632bf70c90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303225} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0510dd4-7330-5414-b48b-4c4f7e0d6ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb41985-a448-5edf-ac16-5140f9df994f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331727" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6ad6bd-ebba-5732-80c2-8d1f7fefd916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302649} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce96722-6380-533c-ba73-d1d4dba3de7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303023} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcb8921-abbb-595e-8400-563faaf3eedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} {:answer/id #uuid "b27edf92-4fc4-5f2b-8e2a-0406162eac16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303563} {:answer/id #uuid "ee01f996-8937-58e6-8682-6e1145ce7e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bcb8921-abbb-595e-8400-563faaf3eedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4ac981-a1ea-5619-b6b7-e06c26ba211c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f3dd1b-d16f-5aa1-9eba-10b4e0fd71e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2459a5ff-007e-530b-8edd-f46a552247c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4a8220-7054-525a-8afa-9194364f34cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303014} {:answer/id #uuid "86c7d469-f99c-5c11-aeb9-bf421146bc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303299} {:answer/id #uuid "923a9172-9e8c-54b3-b41b-41a15e5952e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303364} {:answer/id #uuid "efe9d738-6b6f-52a9-a41b-35b9bd06d1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2459a5ff-007e-530b-8edd-f46a552247c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} :answer-field/id #uuid "c46c2ddf-b5c1-516e-be0e-d85055c76fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8cfe21c-18ac-5f6d-9859-3a5cbe483542" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=5$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0166a0e9-e475-5c8f-b032-9082b7fcde2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302458} {:answer/id #uuid "5314567f-e5df-5ca2-80e8-15b33f649506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302971} {:answer/id #uuid "539ad669-6292-539a-9593-3f19bceb9427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302976} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f64220-da24-5e82-8ca4-f2181aec3314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303555} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd819bc-3592-58f6-8e43-71f8e2d6858e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd819bc-3592-58f6-8e43-71f8e2d6858e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303932} :answer-field/id #uuid "813a5976-379b-5f02-85ae-eb526a514659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e88e764-3128-53c6-9d2a-d1f58dae12a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085f0491-01b7-5bf1-9493-4de4eb3adc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302513} {:answer/id #uuid "53fae90d-e1e5-5e33-bf40-bc28be9e851a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302978} {:answer/id #uuid "9e34058f-f878-5f33-8037-f52171e96c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303441} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dc1111-b6e1-50ea-8948-d3e287173f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303980} {:answer/id #uuid "f4be8e3e-d3ca-5fac-a0d8-1f76a3eab20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fae90d-e1e5-5e33-bf40-bc28be9e851a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302978} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ba6d73-52f7-5082-b9b0-19271c2e615c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b9025a-0c2a-511c-b23e-f617ee7031f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331739" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0346d22e-99ca-51c0-8e3c-59b50a525a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} {:answer/id #uuid "1832ec2d-46da-5208-9b06-d9f7868bef40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302616} {:answer/id #uuid "97ace580-565a-598e-9cb1-40493c663d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303399} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a0b3a0-bdcf-5820-a95c-e2146d10bb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303793} {:answer/id #uuid "db480e54-f880-5865-b053-131cc54e1a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0346d22e-99ca-51c0-8e3c-59b50a525a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5d3bec-6e2b-5dc7-98fa-15f9ea28173f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca0875e1-4252-5a69-bfc7-c43bf6770e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3527a389-4846-52d0-a26e-ab7610b3c975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302786} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae9285b-64e1-54ff-a6e6-ae3a2dd4ef18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302929} {:answer/id #uuid "9079b25c-a77d-5d40-ad19-55fe4a678350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303359} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d4ad9a-fb93-5bbb-be30-9effc80dc1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303554} {:answer/id #uuid "e124e1e6-0f1a-55b2-8382-60e9817db497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e124e1e6-0f1a-55b2-8382-60e9817db497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303860} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec98d2f-0402-5a20-8b48-beacf10636a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26251e42-10da-5a5f-8853-15a03bb82549" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d5c6cb-1c97-55b7-9cac-22add3c7e377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302567} {:answer/id #uuid "192e55d9-a1be-5ba6-9cea-29f23e40a4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302625} {:answer/id #uuid "74223080-19b0-548c-8a50-72e471779141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303164} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d9cab2-ec9a-50f9-ba0f-14cab6570e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303649} {:answer/id #uuid "df283476-20f4-569d-aa03-d80d3e148f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192e55d9-a1be-5ba6-9cea-29f23e40a4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302625} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b760af-2c42-586d-8f5f-8c6c4f28c362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "164f3277-5bbd-5470-9288-18ef536cb19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331974" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f35cdf-6adb-5ac5-9eb7-3ffabd05a2e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302465} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3f4ec4-4119-547d-93c8-de1ae8af98d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302534} {:answer/id #uuid "3deb4ddc-7f6f-500d-a593-973f1dce61d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302844} {:answer/id #uuid "8149fa1b-3362-5f6f-8369-dc6999a85895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303262} {:answer/id #uuid "db6c31dc-cbb3-59af-b486-ecc070980367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3deb4ddc-7f6f-500d-a593-973f1dce61d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302844} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e06dea-4d34-535e-bf2d-4bc51876df02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfb33e06-625f-5894-a78d-d1f418d2b822" :question/math-academy-id "q-331976" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237c21a2-58d9-5482-a65d-954647423b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302680} {:answer/id #uuid "ab460f1b-11f6-526a-9b16-4af30d826eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303510} {:answer/id #uuid "b36e18e5-31bb-5dbf-abee-67b6b6b12210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303570} {:answer/id #uuid "baef488a-08fa-557d-97ca-8a58d1d3de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303611} {:answer/id #uuid "fb50f52d-0998-5637-b066-ecf0170309a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab460f1b-11f6-526a-9b16-4af30d826eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303510} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a682bf-a032-50ac-86b9-905f1dfd7634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0df5f53a-0779-5ce7-90dc-03bd51afb4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332674" :question/problem "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$\n- $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acbc1af-8a14-5ab7-8415-61b9dda79cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305398} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc3cbbe-2e5b-59b6-8a5a-412a02b45ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305404} {:answer/id #uuid "34d3f7e6-b13c-5811-b3e5-a2b5caef1eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305656} {:answer/id #uuid "4d39e25c-6724-5165-bcc1-467f2cb114e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305900} {:answer/id #uuid "674772bb-eb1c-50ea-a9f1-dd10214cf197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d3f7e6-b13c-5811-b3e5-a2b5caef1eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305656} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fa20bb-793d-539d-95f3-08b715ccfaa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30787f50-63d0-5577-9588-239251d45d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333092" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b2af33-606a-518a-a8cf-a8bb610a7b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305201} {:answer/id #uuid "060a171e-7f26-5def-8d3d-13034bb01098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305206} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f4877f-1839-5815-9828-0deb66ddcb34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306733} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa6777-e518-5edf-947f-6d302dcde10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306876} {:answer/id #uuid "d474b0ee-27ca-5e8b-8a77-8ad1c7317178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "060a171e-7f26-5def-8d3d-13034bb01098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305206} :answer-field/id #uuid "911fa9a0-6565-5e5f-a627-64ff80af1793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad56467-9a9c-5515-8f2a-3d6fb0d92b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333311" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2358fabc-35da-5c45-baf8-07b2f30d9c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305488} {:answer/id #uuid "333ea3b2-7f3f-5203-a4b5-6d85b16a2847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305635} {:answer/id #uuid "44c5883c-1e43-5155-b381-97eec704a494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305827} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca81b20-4839-5e14-9ec1-e7a5dc7b8a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306042} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d2decd-0ac2-5f83-bdee-3460714a0bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ca81b20-4839-5e14-9ec1-e7a5dc7b8a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306042} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0e7541-daa3-5d8a-a67a-87297cf4bf96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2271166c-28ab-5bb4-b5b8-f402b78156b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333316" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5$\n- $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4$\n- ${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{4}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}+4y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-2,\\,b(x)=4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddf69d1-8a04-5d1b-99ce-2b182d9bafcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305435} {:answer/id #uuid "811ada5b-7928-5774-b79e-5a9c2ff8afa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306383} {:answer/id #uuid "c41ca1ee-70aa-5c23-886d-93429094afb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306979} {:answer/id #uuid "caebde4d-d2c9-5134-b2e1-db038881d554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307034} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e290cb-1568-58ec-aaa1-2250b6faa322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3e290cb-1568-58ec-aaa1-2250b6faa322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307112} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b8b869-368d-506b-b867-9bccee293cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2cc3f6c-b7da-5ffb-9443-884962200fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333321" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+3,\\,b(x)=-4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51985448-bc1b-59e0-93ed-7a018ee8b30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305946} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc6209a-8a97-5420-b24d-d559c70b2336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306340} {:answer/id #uuid "93ffac01-5204-5239-b9b6-78a8edc3b208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306550} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e4bb01-56d1-500b-b2f0-395619506f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307125} {:answer/id #uuid "fa424b7a-26c5-5220-adbd-eb4c07342c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51985448-bc1b-59e0-93ed-7a018ee8b30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305946} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3e1200-b5e7-5f4c-a02b-68c1496541d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f788c2b2-09c8-57a5-9839-705d54ca27d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333322" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}$\n- $y{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-1,\\,b(x)=2x,\\,c(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation.Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{3}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27327ea8-ce27-5b3e-80d1-a3469c81de6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305517} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbb919f-f596-5390-a734-15dd30acfa32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305771} {:answer/id #uuid "61010ec6-8cd1-5dad-bf8d-1dcd8add9646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306086} {:answer/id #uuid "925f80f4-2747-5d08-906a-4fe00980b955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306535} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf6c9e5-ba76-5da5-a02b-bb30d8c02912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61010ec6-8cd1-5dad-bf8d-1dcd8add9646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306086} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69407ce-3a88-501e-b535-736d132d0233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86209978-f558-5e50-81ba-e94e00e9e46b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333340" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{7}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{7}{3}y,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =3{(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{7}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b4d65d-477c-5947-941e-6499f2f1f6db" :answer/type 