=1.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}y,\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)((1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =5{(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{3}{2}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{3}{2}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=1$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c0c11d-4619-5127-9b7c-ea5346e63bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305360} {:answer/id #uuid "29d71e82-f14a-5349-b979-2127dc4d7a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305551} {:answer/id #uuid "59716f29-4d1c-5f2f-a563-b825e498a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306008} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1a4714-e2a5-597c-86a1-7828bc413720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307199} {:answer/id #uuid "f36da0b7-eaa6-5894-9012-f09ed62cacc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59716f29-4d1c-5f2f-a563-b825e498a9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306008} :answer-field/id #uuid "552aeabf-c688-5ebe-bc78-78c0aa5c4a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bf24239-c81d-5a71-bce2-db470e9ad2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements a